<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Health Connect READ permissions for the record types BADAN ingests.

      Declaring a permission here does NOT request it. `HealthConnectAdapter.permissions` is what
      is actually put in front of the user, and it is requested in staged family bundles rather
      than one blanket prompt (plan §13.1/§14). The manifest is the superset of everything BADAN
      may ever ask for; the adapter decides when. A permission requested by the adapter but
      missing here fails silently at request time, so the two lists are edited together.

      Grouped by consent family below because the families are the unit the user sees and the
      unit Play Data Safety is filled in against — keep a new record type's permission with its
      family rather than appending to the end of the list.
    -->

    <!-- Activity. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_STEPS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_ACTIVE_CALORIES_BURNED" />
    <!--
      READ_TOTAL_CALORIES_BURNED was declared here and is deliberately gone (audit finding #7).
      `HcTypeMap` defers TotalCaloriesBurnedRecord — the registry has no total-expenditure metric,
      and total-minus-active is a derivation rather than an ingestion — so the type never entered
      the app. It also states the governing principle: asking for a permission BADAN does not act
      on spends the user's trust on nothing. The manifest said the opposite of the code, and the
      manifest is what the Play Data Safety form is built from, so the declaration would have had
      BADAN disclosing a data type it never reads.

      Restore this line only in the same change that gives the type a metric to land in.
      `HcTypeMapTest.deferredTypePermissionsAreNotDeclaredInTheManifest` fails if it comes back
      alone.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_DISTANCE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_FLOORS_CLIMBED" />

    <!-- Vitals. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEART_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_RESTING_HEART_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEART_RATE_VARIABILITY" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_RESPIRATORY_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_OXYGEN_SATURATION" />
    <!-- SkinTemperatureRecord is the Health Connect analogue of HealthKit wrist temperature.
         Surfaced as a deviation band, never an absolute clinical temperature (plan §4 rule 9). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_SKIN_TEMPERATURE" />

    <!-- Sleep. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_SLEEP" />

    <!-- Body measurements. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_WEIGHT" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BODY_FAT" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_LEAN_BODY_MASS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_VO2_MAX" />

    <!--
      Glucose (D-26 / ADR-0010 approval A21). A21 approves ingestion of glucose that is ALREADY on
      the platform store — the reading a user's meter or CGM app has written into Health Connect —
      and leaves vendor OAuth (Dexcom/Libre direct) deferred. Health Connect's BloodGlucoseRecord
      is exactly that platform-written path, so this permission is the whole of A21's Android
      surface; there is no second network-reaching integration behind it.

      Declared ahead of its reader by the batch that pre-declares the manifest, so no later feature
      task has to edit this file. `HcTypeMap` has NO BloodGlucoseRecord entry today, which means
      this permission is currently requested by nothing and lands no rows — the manifest's own rule
      three blocks up ("asking for a permission BADAN does not act on spends the user's trust on
      nothing") is therefore in tension with this line until the glucose ingestion task lands its
      `HcTypeMap` entry. If that task does not land, this line must be swept back out rather than
      left to disclose a data type on the Play Data Safety form that never enters the app.

      Note this is invisible to `HcTypeMapTest`: that test polices the two directions it can see —
      requested-but-undeclared, and deferred-entry-but-declared — and a permission with no table
      entry at all is in neither set. The check is this comment and the Batch 4 sweep.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BLOOD_GLUCOSE" />

    <!--
      Workouts. READ_EXERCISE covers ExerciseSessionRecord.

      There is deliberately NO route-read permission here. connect-client 1.1.0 defines only
      WRITE_EXERCISE_ROUTE — reading a route goes through `ExerciseRouteRequestAppContract`
      (action androidx.health.action.REQUEST_EXERCISE_ROUTE), a per-route system consent dialog,
      not a manifest permission. The bulk platform permission (READ_EXERCISE_ROUTES) is
      restricted and requires Google allowlisting, and is not exposed by this client version.
      Verified against the 1.1.0 aar, not assumed. This is also the behavior BADAN wants: routes
      are the one location-bearing health record, and plan §17 gives them their own toggle, so
      per-route consent is the honest shape rather than a limitation to work around. Do not add a
      READ_EXERCISE_ROUTE(S) line "for completeness" — it grants nothing and misstates the ask.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_EXERCISE" />

    <!--
      The Android counterpart to iOS A2 (ADR-0009): without this, Health Connect reads from the
      EPIC-05 WorkManager sync return empty once the app is backgrounded on platforms that
      enforce it, which presents as "background sync runs and finds nothing" rather than as an
      error. Declared here; requested only from the background-sync opt-in, never bundled into
      the first-run prompt. On platforms/HC versions that do not define it, an undefined
      permission is simply never granted — declaring it is inert, not a compatibility risk.

      Note what is NOT here: READ_HEALTH_DATA_HISTORY. BADAN accepts Health Connect's 30-day
      default window and states the achievable window per type in the backfill banner (plan §14)
      rather than asking for a broad historical grant it has not yet earned the user's trust for.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEALTH_DATA_IN_BACKGROUND" />

    <!--
      ============================================================================================
      WRITE permissions — BADAN becomes a WRITER to the platform store. This is a posture change,
      not an implementation detail, and it is flagged here rather than tucked in with the reads.
      ============================================================================================

      Every other line in this file is a READ. The app's own comments above describe a read-only
      relationship with Health Connect ("the record types BADAN ingests"), the privacy policy
      describes a device that receives and keeps, and Play Data Safety has so far been filled in
      against a manifest that only ever asked for other apps' data.

      These four lines change that. A live workout recorded IN BADAN produces a session, heart-rate
      samples, active energy and distance that exist nowhere else; leaving them inside BADAN alone
      would make BADAN the one app on the device whose workouts the rest of the user's health
      picture cannot see — the exact siloing this product exists to argue against. Writing them
      back is the honest resolution, and it is also what iOS's live workout already does through
      HKWorkoutBuilder.

      What has to be true before these are actually requested, none of which is this batch's to
      decide:
        * a decision on file that BADAN writes (there is none yet — D-38's parity mandate implies
          it but does not state it);
        * a Play Data Safety answer that says BADAN shares data with Health Connect;
        * user-facing copy at the point of the ask, since a WRITE grant reads very differently to
          a user than a READ grant does;
        * a rule for what BADAN will and will not write — it must never write a DERIVED number
          (readiness, effort, sleep score) into the platform store, because a derived value with
          BADAN's provisional constants behind it would then propagate into other apps stripped of
          its input manifest, its confidence and its provisional flag. Only measured session data.

      Until then these are declared and unrequested, exactly as the READ set is: declaring is not
      requesting (see the top of this file). If the live-workout task does not land, they get swept
      back out with everything else that never got an implementation.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.WRITE_EXERCISE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.WRITE_HEART_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.WRITE_ACTIVE_CALORIES_BURNED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.WRITE_DISTANCE" />

    <!--
      WorkManager's own manifest already merges this in, so the build works without the line.
      It is stated explicitly anyway: this manifest should read as what BADAN needs, not as what
      it happened to inherit — a reviewer auditing "why can this app run at boot?" should find
      the answer here. Reason: periodic HC sync must survive a reboot, otherwise scheduled work
      silently stops until the user next opens the app.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!--
      EPIC-32. From API 33 (Tiramisu) this is a runtime permission that starts DENIED, so without it
      declared here every notification BADAN posts is silently dropped on a modern device — which is
      how the EPIC-15 automation-pause notice behaved until now. Requested contextually, from the
      Settings → "Summaries & reminders" toggles only (NotificationsSettingsSection.kt), never at
      launch: the ask is the direct consequence of a user opting a class in, and a refusal leaves
      every class off rather than persisting an opt-in the app cannot honour. Everything BADAN posts
      is local, generic and value-free — no health value ever appears on a lock screen.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      Live workout (the Android counterpart to iOS's HKWorkoutSession).

      A workout the user starts in BADAN has to keep sampling while the screen is off and while
      another app is in front, which on Android means a foreground service and nothing else — any
      other substrate is killed mid-session by the process lifecycle. FOREGROUND_SERVICE is the
      base permission; FOREGROUND_SERVICE_HEALTH is the per-type permission Android 14 (API 34)
      requires alongside the `foregroundServiceType="health"` on the <service> below.

      The type is `health`, NOT `specialUse`. `specialUse` requires a written justification
      submitted to and reviewed by Google Play, and is intended for services that genuinely fit no
      declared category — a workout recorder fits `health` exactly, so claiming `specialUse` would
      buy a review cycle in exchange for nothing.

      ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION is here for two reasons that happen to coincide: it is what lets the
      session read step/cadence during the workout, and from API 34 a `health` foreground service
      must hold one of ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION / BODY_SENSORS / HIGH_SAMPLING_RATE_SENSORS at runtime
      or `startForeground` throws. ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION is the one of those three BADAN can justify
      to a user in a sentence.

      What is deliberately NOT here: ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION / ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION, and
      `foregroundServiceType="location"`. v1 mirrors iOS, which excludes GPS from live workouts —
      so a BADAN-recorded workout has no route, the Data Safety story stays "no location collected"
      unchanged, and the one location-bearing record in the app remains the route Health Connect
      hands over per-session under its own consent dialog (see the READ_EXERCISE block above).
      Adding GPS is a product decision with a Data Safety consequence, not a capability to switch
      on because the service type allows it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_HEALTH" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION" />

    <!--
      BLE chest straps / external sensors paired directly to BADAN during a live workout.

      `neverForLocation` is a promise made to the platform, not a hint: it tells Android that BADAN
      will not derive the phone's physical location from scan results, and in exchange the scan
      permission stops implying location access. It is accurate here — BADAN scans to find a heart
      rate strap and for nothing else — and it is what keeps "BADAN collects no location" true on
      the Data Safety form even with a scanner in the app.

      Known gap, stated rather than silently shipped: BLUETOOTH_SCAN/BLUETOOTH_CONNECT are API 31+.
      `minSdk` is 26, so on API 26-30 devices BLE pairing has no permission behind it at all and
      will fail; closing that needs the legacy `BLUETOOTH` / `BLUETOOTH_ADMIN` pair declared with
      `android:maxSdkVersion="30"`. Those lines are not added here because the BLE task has not
      landed and the legacy pair carries its own Play Data Safety wording — whichever task builds
      the scanner adds them, or explicitly raises the feature's floor to API 31.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN"
        android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation"
        tools:targetApi="s" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT" />

    <!--
      Lab-report OCR. D-43 removed the clinical-records ingestion path on iOS after App Review
      refused it, leaving the camera as the lab notebook's third way in alongside a photo already
      in the library and a PDF already on the device. Android inherits that shape, so the camera is
      load-bearing here rather than a convenience: without it the notebook has two import paths,
      not three.

      OCR itself is on-device (ML Kit text recognition, the Android counterpart to iOS's Vision) —
      a photographed lab report never leaves the phone, which is the property that makes pointing a
      camera at a medical document acceptable at all.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      Declaring CAMERA makes Google Play treat `android.hardware.camera` as REQUIRED unless it is
      declared optional here, which would filter BADAN off camera-less devices for a feature that
      is one of three import paths. Same for BLE. Both features degrade honestly when the hardware
      is absent; neither is a reason to make the app uninstallable.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.autofocus" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.bluetooth_le" android:required="false" />

    <!-- ADR-0011 / D-37: keep the Android app offline even when a transitive dependency
         declares INTERNET. REST export remains disabled. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />

    <!-- Provider visibility so getSdkStatus() can detect Health Connect on Android 13 and below. -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.google.android.apps.healthdata" />
        <intent>
            <action android:name="androidx.health.connect.action.SHOW_PERMISSIONS_RATIONALE" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.BADAN">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.BADAN">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Health Connect permissions rationale (Android 13 and below). -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="androidx.health.connect.action.SHOW_PERMISSIONS_RATIONALE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              EPIC-19: static launcher shortcuts. Declared on the LAUNCHER activity because that is
              the one the shortcut menu attaches to. The shortcuts' intents use custom actions
              (net.albayyan.badan.action.*) handled in MainActivity.handleShortcutIntent; they
              target this explicit component, so they need no additional intent-filter here.
            -->
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.shortcuts"
                android:resource="@xml/shortcuts" />
        </activity>

        <!--
          EPIC-19: the "Run export template" quick-settings tile. Android's stand-in for an iOS
          App Intent surface (there is no Shortcuts app to donate to). Opens the Export Center
          rather than exporting headlessly — see RunExportTileService for why that is the honest
          behaviour given there is no notification surface yet to report a background run's outcome.
          BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE is what restricts binding to the system, and exported=true is
          required for the system to bind it at all.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".platform.shortcuts.RunExportTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@drawable/ic_shortcut_export"
            android:label="@string/tile_run_export_label"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          EPIC-05 / D-42 — re-establish the hourly Health Connect sync after a reboot.

          This element is a bug fix, not new surface. `BootReceiver` has existed in the source tree
          since EPIC-05 and has been DEAD the entire time: RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED was declared, the
          class was written, and nothing ever registered it — and a boot receiver is the one kind
          that cannot be registered at runtime, because the process is not alive to do it. The
          class's own KDoc carries the exact XML below and says plainly it is inert without it.
          D-42 names this registration as what makes the receiver live.

          `exported="false"` is correct despite the intent filter: BOOT_COMPLETED is a protected
          broadcast only the system can send, so the receiver needs no external visibility, and an
          exported one would let any app on the device poke BADAN's sync schedule.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".platform.background.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="false"
            android:enabled="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          Live workout foreground service — the Android counterpart to iOS's HKWorkoutSession.

          `foregroundServiceType="health"` (see the FOREGROUND_SERVICE_HEALTH block above for why
          `health` and not `specialUse`, and for why no location type appears here). Not exported:
          nothing outside the app may start or bind a recording session; it is started from the
          app's own UI and stopped from the notification.

          The service is a STUB in this batch — it declares the component so no later task edits
          this manifest, and its class documents exactly what it does not yet do.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".platform.workout.LiveWorkoutService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="health"
            tools:targetApi="34" />

        <!--
          ============================================================================================
          Home-screen widgets — the five kinds iOS ships (EPIC-33).
          ============================================================================================

          Kinds are named after iOS's own widget kind strings
          (`Platform/Ambient/AmbientSnapshotWriter.swift:43` plus EffortRingWidget) so the two
          platforms' widget sets can be diffed by name: ring_single, one_true_sentence, meds_due,
          sleep_last_night, effort_ring.

          Per ADR-0012 these live INSIDE `:app`, not in a separate module or process. iOS's
          `AmbientSnapshotDto` is hand-duplicated across an App Extension boundary because an iOS
          widget is a different process with no call path home; an AppWidget receiver on Android is
          an ordinary BroadcastReceiver in this same APK and this same process, so there is no
          boundary to duplicate a DTO across and none should be invented.

          The receivers below extend `android.appwidget.AppWidgetProvider` — the framework base
          class that `androidx.glance.appwidget.GlanceAppWidgetReceiver` itself extends. The Glance
          artifacts landed on `:app` in the same batch as this file, so the swap is now available;
          it is deliberately not made here because `GlanceAppWidgetReceiver` requires an override
          returning a real `GlanceAppWidget`, i.e. an actual composable widget UI, which belongs to
          the task that builds the widget rather than to the one that declares the component.
          Making that swap changes NOTHING in this manifest: the element, the class name and the
          widget-info metadata are identical for both superclasses, which is the whole point of
          declaring them now.

          `exported="true"` is required — the launcher and the system AppWidget host send
          APPWIDGET_UPDATE to these from outside the app, and an unexported receiver simply never
          receives it. There is no data in a widget-update broadcast for an attacker to supply.

          iOS's Lock Screen / StandBy accessory families have NO Android analogue: keyguard widgets
          were removed from the platform after API 21, so `widgetCategory` is `home_screen` only.
          The nearest real Android surface for glanceable-without-unlocking is the Wear tile set,
          which is why the tile labels are pre-declared in strings.xml alongside these.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".platform.widgets.RingSingleWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_ring_single_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/ring_single_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".platform.widgets.OneTrueSentenceWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_one_true_sentence_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/one_true_sentence_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".platform.widgets.MedsDueWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_meds_due_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/meds_due_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".platform.widgets.SleepLastNightWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_sleep_last_night_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/sleep_last_night_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".platform.widgets.EffortRingWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_effort_ring_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/effort_ring_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <!--
          No Wear data-layer listener service is declared here, and the omission is deliberate
          rather than forgotten.

          A phone-side listener for the Watch->phone refresh nudge (D-34) has to extend
          `com.google.android.gms.wearable.WearableListenerService` and carry a
          `com.google.android.gms.wearable.BIND_LISTENER` filter with the appropriate
          DATA_CHANGED / MESSAGE_RECEIVED data scheme. `play-services-wearable` landed on `:app`
          in the same batch as this file, so the class is now compilable — the reason it is still
          not declared is not a missing dependency.

          It is that the listener is only NEEDED if `:wear`'s transport wakes the phone while the
          app is backgrounded. If the nudge is instead handled by a runtime
          `MessageClient.addListener` registered while the app is in the foreground, a
          manifest-declared service is dead weight that the system binds for nothing. That is a
          `:wear` design question, being answered by the module that owns it, and inventing a
          component here to satisfy the "declare everything up front" rule would mean guessing a
          class name and a package for a service someone else is about to write — producing either
          a duplicate or a registered service that silently receives nothing.

          So this is the ONE component this batch could not pre-declare, and the one manifest edit
          a later task in this port is expected to make. Whoever adds it: it goes here, next to
          this comment, `android:exported="true"` (the Wear framework binds it from outside the
          app) and named for a class under `platform/wear/`.

          Notification channels are absent from this manifest for a different and ordinary reason:
          Android has no manifest element for them. Channels are created in code
          (`platform/notifications/NotificationScheduler.kt`, `AutomationPauseNotifier.kt`); what
          this batch pre-declares for them is their bilingual name/description strings.
        -->

        <!--
          Exports are shared as content:// uris through this provider. Previously they were
          written to external cache and attached as file:// uris, with the app's global StrictMode
          VM policy reset to stop the resulting FileUriExposedException — which merely silenced
          the crash: most share targets on Android 11+ still receive a uri they cannot read, so
          the chooser opened and then silently delivered nothing. `grantUriPermissions` is what
          actually hands the receiving app read access, scoped to the one share.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>

        <!-- Health Connect permissions rationale (Android 14+). -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name="ViewPermissionUsageActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:targetActivity=".MainActivity"
            android:permission="android.permission.START_VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.HEALTH_PERMISSIONS" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>
    </application>
</manifest>
